<b>Introduction</b>: O
Traumatic O
brain O
injury O
(TBI) O
is O
a O
significant O
global O
public O
health O
issue, O
with O
long-term O
impacts O
on O
patients. O
This O
study O
examines O
the O
relationship O
between O
TBI O
severity, O
as O
measured O
by O
the O
Abbreviated O
Injury O
Scale O
(AIS) O
and O
the O
Injury O
Severity O
Score O
(ISS) O
at O
admission, O
and O
various O
sociodemographic, O
clinical, O
and O
injury-related O
factors. O
<b>Methods</b>: O
We O
conducted O
a O
retrospective O
analysis O
using O
data O
from O
164 O
adult O
TBI O
patients. O
All O
were O
admitted O
between O
March O
2020 O
and O
June O
2023 O
to O
two O
Eastern O
European O
tertiary O
hospitals. O
Variables O
included O
sex, O
age, O
education, O
employment, O
marital O
status, O
injury O
type O
and O
cause, O
place O
of O
injury, O
and O
clinical O
measures O
such O
as O
the O
Marshall O
score, O
AIS, O
and O
ISS. O
Statistical O
methods O
included O
Pearson's O
Chi-squared, O
Fisher's O
exact, O
Spearman O
correlation, O
Wilcoxon, O
and O
Kruskal-Wallis O
tests. O
<b>Results</b>: O
Most O
patients O
were O
male O
(65.9%), O
retired O
(59.8%), O
and O
urban O
residents O
(73.8%), O
with O
a O
mean O
age O
of O
64.98 O
years. O
The O
most O
frequent O
mechanism O
of O
injury O
was O
falls O
(76.2%), O
typically O
occurring O
at O
home O
(61%). O
The O
predominant O
injury O
type O
was O
closed O
head O
trauma O
(93.3%). O
Most O
patients O
had O
mild O
AIS O
scores O
(75%), O
and O
the O
mean O
ISS O
was O
6.52 O
(SD: O
4.55). O
Statistically O
significant O
group O
differences O
were O
found O
for O
AIS O
among O
categories O
of O
Modified O
Marshall O
Score, O
injury O
type, O
and O
education O
categories O
and O
for O
ISS O
among O
categories O
of O
the O
Modified O
Marshall O
Score, O
injury O
type, O
cause O
and O
place O
of O
injury, O
employment O
status, O
and O
sex. O
No O
significant O
correlations O
were O
found O
between O
AIS O
or O
ISS O
and O
age O
or O
hospital O
length O
of O
stay. O
<b>Conclusions</b>: O
AIS O
is O
more O
anatomically O
focused. O
ISS O
reflects O
broader O
systemic O
injury O
patterns O
and O
is O
more O
influenced O
by O
contextual O
factors. O
These O
findings O
are O
particularly O
relevant O
for O
the O
Eastern O
European O
population O
and O
can O
help O
develop O
better O
healthcare O
policies O
for O
the O
region. O

Alzheimer's O
disease O
(AD) O
is O
the O
leading O
cause O
of O
dementia O
worldwide O
and O
is O
becoming O
one O
of O
the O
most O
morbid O
diseases O
of O
this O
century. O
Recently, O
