A O
decrease O
in O
visual O
acuity, O
contrast O
sensitivity, O
and O
color O
vision O
has O
been O
observed, O
primarily O
associated O
with O
retinal O
ganglion O
cell O
loss O
or O
damage. O
Furthermore, O
alterations O
in O
the O
visual O
field, O
ocular O
motility, O
and O
visual O
perception O
have O
been O
recorded, O
mainly O
resulting O
from O
cortical O
changes. O
These O
optical O
parameters O
frequently O
correlate O
with O
patients' O
cognitive O
status. O
In O
conclusion, O
these O
findings O
highlight O
the O
importance O
of O
developing O
strategies O
to O
preserve O
visual O
function O
in O
these O
patients, O
helping O
to O
prevent O
further O
deterioration O
in O
their O
quality O
of O
life, O
and O
emphasize O
the O
potential O
of O
visual O
function O
assessment O
as O
a O
tool O
for O
diagnosis O
or O
predicting O
AD O
progression. O

Striatum O
can O
be O
described O
as O
a O
brain O
region O
containing O
a O
general O
neuronal O
mechanism O
to O
associate O
actions O
or O
events O
with O
reward. O
In O
particular, O
neural O
activity O
in O
the O
human O
striatum O
is O
modulated O
by O
social O
actions O
and, O
critically, O
by O
the O
conjunction O
of O
social O
actions O
and O
own O
