of O
life-sustaining O
therapy O
(WLST) O
in O
the O
first O
weeks. O
However, O
long-term O
neurological O
outcomes O
rarely O
reach O
the O
ICU O
team, O
limiting O
the O
opportunity O
to O
learn O
from O
decisions. O
This O
study O
aimed O
to O
assess O
the O
availability O
of O
long-term O
neurological O
outcome O
data O
and O
from O
which O
sources O
this O
could O
be O
retrieved.MethodsThis O
single-center O
retrospective O
observational O
study O
included O
all O
ICU O
survivors O
with O
SABI O
between O
January O
first O
and O
December O
31<sup>st</sup> O
2022. O
Patient O
characteristics O
and O
neurological O
outcome O
data O
were O
extracted O
from O
the O
patient O
records. O
Neurological O
outcome O
was O
assessed O
using O
the O
Glasgow O
Outcome O
Scale O
Extended O
(GOSE) O
within O
6 O
months O
after O
admission.ResultsA O
total O
of O
65 O
patients O
were O
included, O
the O
median O
Glasgow O
Coma O
Scale O
(GCS) O
score O
at O
admission O
was O
6 O
[IQR O
4-7], O
the O
most O
common O
admission O
diagnosis O
was O
subarachnoid O
hemorrhage O
(34%). O
The O
mean O
ICU O
stay O
was O
8 O
days O
(SD O
8) O
and O
hospital O
stay O
38 O
days O
(SD O
29). O
Outcome O
data O
could O
be O
retrieved O
from O
neurology O
or O
neurosurgery O
outpatient O
clinic O
notes O
in O
54% O
of O
patients, O
in O
12% O
from O
the O
post-ICU O
clinic O
and O
in O
2% O
from O
other O
hospitals, O
rehabilitation O
facilities O
and O
nursing O
homes. O
Mortality O
within O
6 O
months O
was O
26%. O
A O
favorable O
GOSE O
score O
(≥ O
5) O
was O
found O
in O
18 O
patients O
(28%), O
an O
unfavorable O
score O
in O
42 O
patients O
(64%), O
and O
in O
5 O
patients O
(8%) O
no O
outcome O
could O
be O
determined.ConclusionsFunctional O
recovery O
outcomes O
could O
be O
extracted O
from O
the O
patient O
records O
in O
nearly O
all O
ICU O
survivors O
with O
SABI. O
However, O
these O
outcomes O
were O
mostly O
identified O
from O
outpatient O
clinic O
notes O
from O
the O
neurology O
and O
neurosurgery O
department O
and O
it O
is O
uncertain O
whether O
these O
outcomes O
reach O
ICU O
physicians. O
A O
clear O
feedback O
loop O
is O
needed, O
to O
ensure O
ICU O
teams O
can O
learn O
from O
long-term O
patient O
trajectories O
and O
improve O
future O
patient O
care. O

IntroductionHuntington's O
Disease O
(HD) O
is O
a O
rare O
neurodegenerative O
disease O
that O
profoundly O
affects O
both O
individuals O
diagnosed O
with O
the O
condition O
and O
their O
caregivers. O
This O
review O
aims O
to O
examine O
the O
burden O
experienced O
by O
informal O
caregivers O
of O
patients O
with O
HD O
and O
identify O
relevant O
factors O
that O
exacerbate O
or O
mitigate O
this O
burden.MethodsThe O
PRISMA O
guidelines O
were O
followed, O
and O
an O
extensive O
search O
of O
electronic O
databases O
(PubMed, O
Science O
Direct, O
Taylor O
& O
Francis) O
was O
undertaken O
to O
identify O
original O
research O
articles O
published O
in O
English O
between O
January O
2005 O
and O
April O
2025. O
Two O
reviewers O
independently O
screened O
the O
studies. O
The O
quality O
of O
the O
studies O
was O
evaluated O
using O
the O
Critical O
Appraisal O
Program O
(CASP). O
Data O
were O
extracted, O
and O
a O
narrative O
synthesis O
was O
conducted O
to O
integrate O
and O
summarize O
the O
results.ResultsTwelve O
studies O
were O
included O
in O
the O
review O
involving O
569 O
caregivers O
of O
patients O
with O
HD. O
Studies O
were O
conducted O
in O
Europe, O
the O
United O
States, O
Canada, O
and O
Australia, O
with O
one O
taking O
place O
in O
South O
Korea. O
Patient O
demographics, O
caregiver O
characteristics, O
disease-related O
factors, O
disrupted O
family O
dynamics, O
caregivers' O
compromised O
mental O
health, O
and O
availability O
to O
access O
networks O
are O
related O
to O
caregiver O
burden. O
Neuropsychiatric O
symptoms O
and O
the O
hereditary O
nature O
of O
the O
disease O
have O
been O
identified O
as O
important O
correlates O
of O
caregiver O
strain.ConclusionCaring O
for O
individuals O
with O
