The O
human O
brain O
is O
a O
large-scale O
network, O
containing O
multiple O
segregated, O
functionally O
specialized O
systems. O
With O
increasing O
age, O
these O
systems O
become O
less O
segregated, O
but O
the O
reasons O
and O
consequences O
of O
this O
age-related O
reorganization O
are O
largely O
unknown. O
Thus, O
after O
characterizing O
age- O
and O
sex-specific O
differences O
in O
the O
segregation O
of O
global, O
sensorimotor, O
and O
association O
systems O
using O
resting-state O
functional O
MRI O
data, O
we O
analyzed O
how O
segregation O
relates O
to O
cognitive O
performance O
in O
both O
classical O
and O
eye O
movement O
tasks O
across O
age O
strata O
and O
whether O
this O
is O
influenced O
by O
the O
degree O
of O
neuropathology. O
Our O
analyses O
included O
6,455 O
participants O
(30-95 O
years) O
of O
the O
community-based O
Rhineland O
Study. O
System O
segregation O
indices O
were O
based O
on O
functional O
connectivity O
within O
and O
between O
12 O
brain O
systems. O
We O
assessed O
cognitive O
performance O
with O
tests O
for O
memory, O
processing O
speed, O
executive O
function, O
and O
crystallized O
intelligence O
and O
oculomotor O
tasks. O
Multivariable O
regression O
models O
confirmed O
that O
brain O
systems O
become O
less O
segregated O
with O
age O
(e.g., O
global O
segregation: O
standardized O
regression O
coefficient O
(ß) O
= O
-0.298; O
95% O
confidence O
interval O
[-0.299, O
-0.297], O
p O
< O
0.001) O
and O
that O
in O
older O
age O
this O
effect O
is O
stronger O
in O
women O
compared O
to O
men. O
Higher O
segregation O
benefited O
memory O
(especially O
in O
young O
individuals) O
and O
processing O
speed O
in O
individuals O
with O
mild O
neuropathology O
(not O
significant O
after O
multiple O
testing O
correction). O
<i>Lower</i> O
segregation O
benefited O
crystallized O
intelligence O
in O
46- O
to O
55-year-olds. O
Associations O
between O
segregation O
indices O
and O
cognition O
were O
generally O
weak O
(ß O
~ O
0.01-0.06). O
This O
suggests O
that O
optimal O
brain O
organization O
may O
depend O
on O
the O
degree O
of O
brain O
pathology. O
Age-related O
brain O
reorganization O
could O
serve O
as O
a O
compensatory O
mechanism O
and O
partly O
explain O
improvements O
in O
crystallized O
intelligence O
and O
the O
decline O
in O
fluid O
cognitive O
domains O
from O
adolescence O
to O
(late) O
adulthood. O

