Mental O
fatigue O
is O
a O
significant O
psychopathological O
symptom O
that O
has O
recently O
gained O
attention, O
particularly O
in O
chronic O
fatigue O
syndrome/myalgic O
encephalomyelitis O
and O
Post-COVID-19 O
condition. O
However, O
fatigue O
is O
a O
clinically O
relevant O
symptom O
across O
a O
wide O
range O
of O
mental O
and O
neurological O
disorders. O
To O
identify O
a O
transdiagnostic O
functional O
network O
associated O
with O
fatigue, O
we O
conducted O
an O
activation O
likelihood O
estimation O
meta-analysis O
of O
neuroimaging O
studies. O
The O
primary O
inclusion O
criterion O
was O
studies O
involving O
any O
medical O
condition O
where O
patients O
exhibited O
significantly O
higher O
levels O
of O
fatigue O
compared O
to O
healthy O
controls. O
A O
systematic O
literature O
review O
across O
three O
major O
scientific O
databases O
identified O
46 O
eligible O
neuroimaging O
studies, O
including O
a O
total O
of O
2603 O
individuals. O
The O
meta-analysis O
of O
these O
studies O
revealed O
a O
widespread O
cortical-subcortical O
network O
involving O
frontal, O
limbic, O
basal O
ganglia O
and O
parietal O
structures. O
Three O
main O
clusters O
were O
highlighted: O
a O
frontal-striatal-limbic O
cluster, O
a O
frontal-cingulate O
cluster O
and O
a O
parietal O
cluster, O
with O
regions O
implicated O
in O
cognitive, O
emotional O
and O
somatosensory O
symptoms O
associated O
with O
mental O
fatigue. O
Quality O
analysis O
indicated O
a O
moderate O
risk O
of O
bias O
in O
the O
majority O
of O
the O
included O
studies. O
Overall, O
our O
findings O
provide O
scientific O
evidence O
for O
a O
transdiagnostic O
mental O
