that O
accompanies O
ELA O
by O
addressing O
oxidative O
stress. O
Treatment O
with O
the O
antioxidant O
MitoQ O
began O
at O
weaning O
and O
extended O
to O
3 O
months. O
Measures O
of O
complex O
I O
activity O
demonstrated O
full O
recovery O
in O
adults. O
Female-specific O
deficits O
in O
the O
sucrose O
preference O
task, O
which O
is O
a O
measure O
of O
rewarding O
behavior O
in O
rodents, O
also O
exhibited O
recovery, O
with O
preference O
for O
sucrose O
comparable O
to O
that O
of O
CAU O
mice. O
These O
data O
indicate O
that O
mitochondrial O
health O
is O
one O
component O
of O
responses O
to O
early O
life O
adversity O
that O
has O
lifespan O
implications, O
but O
with O
the O
capacity O
to O
recover O
normal O
functioning O
in O
adults.<b>Significance O
Statement</b> O
Chronic O
early O
life O
stress O
in O
humans O
and O
animals O
leads O
to O
enduring O
functional O
changes O
to O
brain O
circuitry O
and O
behavior. O
An O
emerging O
concept O
involves O
the O
adaptation O
of O
mitochondria O
to O
address O
new O
energy O
demands O
as O
a O
primary O
cellular O
response O
to O
chronic O
stress. O
Long-term O
disruptions O
lead O
to O
oxidative O
stress, O
anhedonia O
and O
cognitive O
deficits. O
To O
determine O
if O
direct O
treatment O
of O
oxidative O
stress O
can O
mitigate O
adult O
dysfunction, O
an O
8-week O
regimen O
of O
an O
antioxidant O
was O
administered O
to O
post-weaning O
mice O
that O
were O
raised O
early O
postnatally O
in O
an O
environment O
that O
disrupts O
predictable O
maternal O
care. O
Complex O
I O
mitochondrial O
energetics O
and O
sucrose O
preference O
were O
restored O
to O
levels O
comparable O
to O
mice O
raised O
in O
care O
as O
usual O
conditions, O
demonstrating O
a O
link O
between O
mitochondrial O
health O
and O
functional O
outcomes. O

Zinc O
is O
