the O
TOS O
condition, O
reflecting O
heightened O
attention O
to O
threatening O
stimuli. O
In O
contrast, O
the O
N400 O
component O
showed O
significant O
differences O
between O
moral O
and O
immoral O
acts O
only O
in O
the O
safe O
condition; O
this O
distinction O
was O
absent O
in O
the O
TOS O
condition, O
indicating O
impaired O
semantic O
processing O
under O
anxiety. O
Together, O
these O
findings O
demonstrate O
that O
threat-induced O
anxiety O
disrupts O
moral O
judgment O
processes, O
leading O
to O
reduced O
sensitivity O
to O
immoral O
behaviors. O
This O
highlights O
the O
critical O
role O
of O
anxiety O
in O
moral O
processing O
and O
the O
flexibility O
and O
context-dependence O
of O
moral O
judgments. O

The O
legalization O
of O
cannabis O
in O
several O
states O
across O
the O
United O
States O
has O
increased O
the O
need O
to O
better O
understand O
its O
effects O
on O
the O
body, O
brain, O
and O
behavior, O
particularly O
in O
different O
populations. O
Previous O
rodent O
studies O
have O
revealed O
age O
and O
sex O
differences O
in O
response O
to O
injected O
Δ<sup>9</sup>-tetrahydrocannabinol O
(THC). O
However, O
the O
pharmacokinetic O
and O
pharmacodynamic O
properties O
of O
THC O
administered O
through O
more O
translationally O
relevant O
routes O
of O
administration O
are O
less O
well O
known. O
Here, O
we O
addressed O
this O
gap O
by O
investigating O
age O
and O
sex O
differences O
in O
pharmacokinetics O
and O
the O
acute O
behavioral O
effects O
of O
vaporized O
cannabis O
e-liquid O
in O
mice. O
Adolescent O
(postnatal O
day O
[P] O
35-50) O
and O
adult O
(≥P70) O
mice O
of O
both O
sexes O
received O
noncontingent O
exposure O
to O
vehicle O
vapor, O
150 O
mg/mL O
(CAN150), O
or O
300 O
mg/mL O
(CAN300) O
vaporized O
cannabis O
extract O
diluted O
in O
either O
80% O
propylene O
glycol/20% O
vegetable O
glycerin O
(PG/VG) O
or O
100% O
polyethylene O
glycol O
400 O
(PEG). O
Immediately O
after O
exposure, O
body O
temperature, O
hot O
plate O
withdrawal O
latency, O
and O
locomotion O
were O
assessed. O
Blood O
was O
collected O
at O
0, O
30, O
and O
60 O
min O
after O
vapor O
exposure, O
and O
plasma O
THC, O
11-hydroxy-THC, O
and O
11-nor-9-carboxy-THC O
were O
analyzed. O
THC O
concentrations O
were O
higher O
in O
both O
the O
plasma O
of O
vapor-exposed O
mice O
and O
the O
cannabis O
extract O
solutions O
when O
PEG O
was O
the O
carrier O
oil O
compared O
with O
PG/VG. O
Vaporized O
cannabis O
(mixed O
with O
PEG) O
at O
the O
highest O
dose O
