Caffeine O
is O
the O
most O
commonly O
used O
substance O
during O
gaming O
sessions. O
Despite O
health O
guidelines O
to O
avoid O
caffeine O
before O
adulthood, O
many O
adolescents O
use O
caffeine O
to O
compensate O
for O
lost O
sleep O
or O
prolong O
wakefulness O
to O
enhance O
gaming O
performance. O
The O
relationship O
between O
gaming O
and O
sleep O
is O
well-established, O
but O
the O
role O
of O
caffeine O
has O
been O
under-explored. O
This O
study O
investigated O
the O
potential O
mediating O
effect O
of O
caffeine O
use O
on O
the O
relationship O
between O
gaming O
duration/problems O
and O
sleep O
duration/difficulties O
in O
young O
adolescents. O
The O
Years O
2, O
3, O
and O
4 O
follow-up O
data O
from O
the O
Adolescent O
Brain O
Cognitive O
Development O
study O
were O
analyzed O
(N O
= O
2,749, O
Mage O
= O
11.9 O
at O
Year O
2). O
Data O
from O
questionnaires O
on O
daily O
gaming O
duration O
and O
problematic O
gaming O
(Year O
2), O
weekly O
caffeine O
use O
(Year O
3), O
and O
daily O
sleep O
duration O
and O
sleep O
difficulties O
(Year O
4) O
were O
examined. O
Analyses O
were O
conducted O
using O
an O
R O
package O
for O
causal O
mediation O
analysis. O
Caffeine O
use O
partially O
mediated O
the O
positive O
relationship O
between O
gaming O
hours/problems O
and O
sleep O
difficulties O
and O
the O
negative O
relationship O
between O
gaming O
hours/problems O
and O
sleep O
duration. O
The O
mediating O
effect O
of O
caffeine O
use O
was O
small O
but O
statistically O
significant O
in O
the O
relationship O
between O
gaming O
duration/problems O
and O
sleep O
duration/difficulties O
in O
young O
adolescents. O
Given O
the O
widespread O
use O
of O
caffeine O
among O
adolescents O
who O
game O
and O
the O
potential O
impact O
of O
modifying O
consumption O
during O
development, O
caffeine O
use O
could O
potentially O
be O
targeted O
to O
reduce O
sleep-related O
harm O
and O
other O
associated O
health O
issues. O

Typically, O
people O
perform O
actions O
in O
a O
valenced-positive O
or O
negative-way, O
depending O
on O
their O
attitudes O
or O
desires. O
These O
forms O
of O
action O
are O
named O
vitality O
forms O
(VFs). O
While O
it O
is O
well O
established O
that O
action O
goals O
are O
mediated O
by O
a O
parieto-frontal O
network, O
less O
is O
known O
about O
the O
processing O
of O
VFs. O
Recent O
fMRI O
studies O
suggest O
that O
the O
insula B-Brain
(INS)-and O
its O
connections O
with O
the O
parieto-frontal O
circuit-plays O
a O
crucial O
role O
in O
VFs O
processing. O
However, O
a O
key O
question O
remains: O
How O
does O
our O
internal O
affective O
state O
shape O
our O
motor O
behavior? O
To O
explore O
this O
issue, O
we O
conducted O
an O
fMRI O
study. O
Participants O
were O
required O
to O
perform O
two O
sequential O
tasks: O
1) O
to O
evoke O
either O
a O
positive O
(enthusiastic) O
or O
a O
negative O
(angry) O
affective O
state O
(feeling O
task); O
2) O
to O
execute O
an O
action O
while O
maintaining O
these O
affective O
states O
(execution O
task). O
Univariate O
analysis O
revealed O
activation O
of O
the O
INS O
and O
dorsolateral O
prefrontal O
cortex O
(PFC) O
during O
the O
feeling O
task, O
which O
extended O
to O
the O
premotor O
(PM) O
and O
parietal O
areas O
during O
the O
execution O
task. O
To O
determine O
the O
directionality O
of O
information O
flow O
among O
these O
nodes, O
we O
employed O
dynamic O
causal O
modeling. O
Bayesian O
model O
comparison O
showed O
that, O
during O
the O
feeling O
task, O
affect O
generation O
involves O
INS, O
which, O
together O
with O
the O
PFC, O
modulates O
the O
activity O
of O
PM. O
In O
contrast, O
during O
execution, O
motor O
commands O
emerge O
from O
PM O
and O
influence O
activity O
in O
the O
INS O
and O
PFC. O
These O
findings O
indicate O
that O
while O
the O
internal O
states O
crucially O
imply O
the O
INS, O
their O
regulation O
is O
mediated O
by O
PFC. O
The O
PM O
cortex O
plays O
a O
crucial O
role O
in O
the O
selection O
of O
the O
corresponding O
action O
VFs. O

Interpretation O
and O
analysis O
of O
magnetic O
resonance O
imaging O
(MRI) O
scans O
in O
clinical O
settings O
comprise O
time-consuming O
