the O
specialized O
functions O
of O
cholinergic O
neurons O
across O
different O
circuits, O
especially O
by O
modulating O
CGRP, O
CCK, O
or O
calbindin. O
These O
results O
could O
provide O
new O
insight O
into O
cholinergic O
modulation O
throughout O
the O
BFCN O
and O
striatum. O

This O
study O
examined O
the O
association O
between O
adherence O
to O
the O
Dutch O
MIND O
diet O
(Mediterranean-DASH O
Intervention O
for O
Neurodegenerative O
Delay, O
MIND-NL) O
and O
the O
Dutch O
dietary O
guidelines O
(DHD2015-index) O
with O
global O
cognitive O
function O
in O
older O
adults O
at O
risk O
of O
cognitive O
decline. O
A O
cross-sectional O
study O
was O
conducted O
using O
baseline O
data O
of O
the O
FINGER-NL O
trial. O
A O
total O
of O
1,135 O
older O
adults, O
aged O
60-80 O
years, O
at O
risk O
for O
cognitive O
decline O
with O
complete O
dietary O
data O
and O
complete O
neuropsychological O
tests O
were O
included O
in O
the O
analyses. O
A O
validated O
72-item O
Food O
Frequency O
Questionnaire O
(FFQ) O
was O
used O
to O
assess O
adherence O
to O
the O
dietary O
patterns. O
Global O
cognitive O
function O
was O
assessed O
by O
calculating O
a O
composite O
score O
based O
on O
four O
subtests O
of O
a O
neuropsychological O
test O
battery. O
Multiple O
linear O
regression O
analyses, O
adjusted O
for O
age, O
sex, O
education O
level, O
socioeconomic O
status O
(SES), O
body O
mass O
index O
(BMI), O
physical O
activity O
level, O
smoking O
status, O
and O
cardiovascular O
risk O
factors, O
were O
applied O
to O
examine O
potential O
associations O
between O
MIND-NL O
diet O
score O
and O
global O
cognitive O
function, O
and O
between O
DHD2015-index O
and O
global O
cognitive O
function. O
Interaction O
and O
subsequent O
subgroup O
analyses O
were O
conducted O
based O
on O
age, O
sex, O
education, O
SES, O
and O
physical O
activity. O
Explorative O
network O
analyses O
were O
applied O
to O
identify O
links O
between O
individual O
dietary O
intake O
components O
and O
global O
cognitive O
function. O
The O
median O
[IQR] O
age O
of O
the O
participants O
was O
67 O
[64-71] O
years. O
Overall, O
neither O
the O
MIND-NL O
diet O
nor O
the O
DHD2015-index O
was O
associated O
with O
the O
global O
cognition O
composite O
score O
(β O
= O
0.014, O
95%CI: O
-0.016, O
0.045, O
p O
= O
0.35 O
and O
β O
= O
0.003, O
95%CI: O
-0.000, O
0.006, O
p O
= O
0.07, O
respectively). O
The O
association O
between O
MIND-NL O
diet O
score O
and O
global O
cognition O
was O
moderated O
by O
age O
(p<sub>interaction</sub> O
= O
0.06), O
with O
adults O
under O
70 O
years O
of O
age O
showing O
a O
positive O
trend. O
Although O
no O
significant O
interaction O
was O
noted O
(p<sub>interaction</sub> O
= O
0.28), O
an O
association O
was O
found O
between O
DHD2015-index O
and O
global O
cognition O
in O
participants O
aged O
under O
70 O
years O
(β O
= O
0.004, O
95%CI: O
0.000, O
0.008, O
p O
= O
0.048). O
Dietary O
intake O
of O
fruiting O
vegetables O
and O
vitamin O
E O
were O
positively O
correlated O
with O
global O
cognitive O
function. O
In O
this O
study, O
adherence O
to O
the O
Dutch O
dietary O
guidelines O
was O
associated O
with O
better O
global O
cognitive O
function O
among O
older O
adults O
under O
the O
age O
of O
70 O
years O
at O
risk O
of O
cognitive O
decline. O
Future O
research O
aims O
at O
investigating O
longitudinal O
associations O
and O
confirming O
the O
moderating O
effect O
of O
age. O

Myelitis O
is O
a O
relatively O
common O
clinical O
entity O
for O
neurologists, O
with O
diverse O
underlying O
causes. O
The O
aim O
of O
this O
study O
was O
to O
describe O
the O
incidence O
of O
myelitis, O
its O
causes, O
clinical O
presentation, O
and O
factors O
predicting O
functional O
outcomes O
and O
relapses. O
Using O
the O
Swedish O
National O
Patient O
Registry, O
we O
identified O
all O
adult O
patients O
in O
Stockholm O
County O
between O
2008 O
and O
2018 O
using O
International O
Classification O
of O
Diseases, O
10th O
Edition O
(ICD-10) O
codes O
likely O
to O
include O
myelitis. O
We O
collected O
medical O
records O
and O
classified O
patients O
using O
a O
modification O
of O
the O
2002 O
Transverse O
Myelitis O
Consortium O
Group O
criteria. O
Long-term O
follow-up O
data O
were O
collected O
for O
patients O
not O
diagnosed O
with O
multiple O
sclerosis O
(MS) O
or O
neuromyelitis O
optica O
spectrum O
disorder O
as O
a O
result O
of O
the O
initial O
myelitis. O
We O
identified O
2,321 O
individuals, O
of O
whom O
461 O
were O
patients O
with O
myelitis. O
The O
crude O
mean O
incidence O
of O
all-cause O
myelitis O
was O
24.9 O
(95% O
CI O
16.7-33.9) O
cases O
per O
million O
person-years, O
of O
which O
idiopathic O
myelitis O
had O
an O
incidence O
of O
8.0 O
(95% O
CI O
3.8-12.1) O
cases O
per O
million O
person-years. O
Partial O
myelitis O
was O
found O
in O
80% O
of O
patients. O
Poor O
functional O
outcome O
was O
found O
in O
11% O
of O
the O
cohort O
and O
correlated, O
in O
a O
multivariate O
logistic O
model, O
with O
age O
older O
than O
50 O
years O
(OR O
4.26, O
95% O
CI O
1.75-10.40), O
transverse O
spinal O
cord O
lesions O
(odds O
ratio O
[OR] O
6.85, O
95% O
CI O
2.68-17.52), O
elevated O
CSF O
count O
of O
polymorphonuclear O
cells O
(OR O
6.09, O
95% O
CI O
1.56-23.72), O
and O
elevated O
CSF/serum O
albumin O
ratio O
(OR O
3.17, O
95% O
CI O
1.23-8.17). O
The O
median O
follow-up O
time O
was O
5.4 O
years. O
Relapses O
occurred O
in O
27% O
of O
patients O
with O
idiopathic O
myelitis O
and O
72% O
of O
patients O
with O
unspecified O
demyelinating O
disease O
of O
the O
CNS. O
An O
increased O
relapse O
rate O
after O
idiopathic O
myelitis O
was O
found O
to O
be O
associated, O
in O
a O
multivariate O
model, O
with O
the O
presence O
of O
oligoclonal O
bands O
(incidence O
rate O
ratio O
[IRR] O
4.47, O
95% O
CI O
1.70-11.73), O
transverse O
spinal O
cord O
lesions O
(IRR O
2.81, O
95% O
CI O
1.11-7.12), O
and O
multifocal O
spinal O
cord O
lesions O
(IRR O
2.82, O
95% O
CI O
1.03-7.69). O
Around O
half O
(48%) O
of O
all O
patients O
with O
myelitis O
received O
MS O
diagnosis O
during O
the O
study O
period. O
This O
large O
population-wide O
study O
describes O
a O
relatively O
high O
incidence O
of O
myelitis O
and O
low O
risk O
of O
relapses O
after O
idiopathic O
myelitis. O
A O
complete O
diagnostic O
workup O
of O
myelitis, O
including O
MRI O
of O
the O
entire O
CNS O
and O
collection O
of O
CSF, O
is O
essential O
in O
evaluating O
underlying O
causes O
and O
prognosis. O

The O
lateral O
prefrontal O
cortex O
(LPFC) O
serves O
as O
a O
critical O
hub O
for O
higher-order O
cognitive O
and O
executive O
functions O
in O
the O
human O
brain, O
coordinating O
brain O
networks O
whose O
disruption O
has O
been O
implicated O
in O
many O
neurological O
and O
psychiatric O
disorders. O
While O
transcranial O
brain O
stimulation O
treatments O
often O
target O
the O
LPFC, O
our O
current O
understanding O
of O
connectivity O
profiles O
guiding O
these O
interventions O
based O
on O
electrophysiology O
remains O
limited. O
Here, O
we O
present O
a O
high-resolution O
probabilistic O
map O
of O
bidirectional O
effective O
connectivity O
between O
the O
LPFC O
and O
widespread O
cortical O
and O
subcortical O
regions. O
This O
map O
is O
derived O
from O
intracranial O
evoked O
potential O
analysis O
of O
48,797 O
intracranial O
direct O
electrical O
stimulation O
runs O
across O
759 O
implantations O
in O
724 O
patients O
with O
refractory O
epilepsy O
(368 O
male, O
354 O
female, O
two O
unspecified; O
mean O
age O
24±13.5 O
years). O
We O
mapped O
probabilistic O
connectivity O
between O
brain O
parcels O
with O
adaptive O
resolution O
- O
higher O
resolution O
in O
the O
LPFC O
in O
the O
hemisphere O
of O
interest O
and O
lower O
elsewhere O
- O
maintaining O
statistical O
power O
while O
achieving O
95% O
average O
confidence O
interval O
of O
∼0.03 O
for O
connectivity O
probability O
estimates. O
In O
addition, O
the O
significance O
threshold O
(p-value) O
for O
probabilistic O
connectivity O
was O
obtained O
from O
surrogate O
distributions. O
Overall, O
we O
observed O
remarkable O
symmetry O
between O
afferent O
and O
efferent O
connectivity O
patterns O
of O
the O
LPFC, O
with O
a O
slight O
preference O
for O
efferent O
connections O
(mean O
slope O
= O
0.92±0.09, O
mean O
R² O
= O
0.93±0.025). O
For O
example, O
connections O
between O
the O
inferior O
frontal O
gyrus O
(IFG) O
and O
anterior O
cingulate O
showed O
notable O
directional O
asymmetry. O
The O
IFG O
strongly O
projected O
to O
most O
brain O
networks O
compared O
to O
other O
LPFC O
regions, O
with O
the O
strongest O
connectivity O
to O
the O
ventral O
attention O
network O
(0.26±0.01 O
compared O
to O
values O
between O
0.15 O
and O
0.21 O
in O
other O
LPFC O
regions). O
Posterior O
DLPFC O
demonstrated O
stronger O
connectivity O
to O
brain O
networks O
compared O
to O
anterior O
DLPFC O
regions O
(eg. O
0.21±0.01 O
vs O
0.15±0.01 O
for O
connectivity O
to O
ventral O
attention O
network), O
with O
the O
exception O
of O
the O
limbic O
cortex. O
All O
LPFC O
subregions O
strongly O
projected O
to O
the O
fronto-parietal O
(greater O
than O
0.17) O
and O
ventral O
attention O
(greater O
than O
0.15) O
networks, O
with O
moderate O
connections O
to O
the O
default O
network O
(between O
0.1 O
and O
0.15, O
with O
the O
maximum O
corresponding O
to O
superior O
DLPFC). O
Finally, O
latency O
analysis O
suggested O
that O
the O
left O
LPFC's O
influence O
on O
ipsilateral O
emotion-related O
regions O
is O
primarily O
polysynaptic, O
with O
particularly O
strong O
pathways O
from O
IFG O
to O
amygdala B-Brain
(0.16±0.02) O
and O
hippocampus B-Brain
(0.12±0.01). O
Taken O
together, O
these O
comprehensive O
connectivity O
maps O
provide O
a O
new O
detailed O
electrophysiological O
foundation O
for O
understanding O
the O
functional O
anatomy O
of O
LPFC O
and O
guiding O
targeted O
brain O
stimulation O
protocols. O

BackgroundThe O
production O
of O
verbal O
tenses O
is O
impaired O
in O
people O
with O
Alzheimer's O
disease O
(AD), O
as O
shown O
by O
several O
studies O
focusing O
on O
time O
reference O
and O
using O
sentence O
completion O
tasks. O
However, O
there O
is O
currently O
a O
limited O
understanding O
of O
how O
tense O
is O
produced O
in O
discourse O
with O
this O
disease. O
Discourse O
is O
interesting O
as O
it O
involves O
building O
a O
mental O
representation O
of O
the O
event O
to O
be O
narrated O
with O
its O
temporal O
framework O
and O
translating O
this O
framework O
into O
language O
using O
tense. O
A O
shift O
from O
the O
present O
perspective O
to O
the O
time O
of O
the O
event O
is O
required. O
This O
may O
be O
particularly O
difficult O
for O
people O
with O
AD, O
who O
also O
show O
an O
alteration O
in O
mental O
time O
travel O
capacity, O
which O
is O
linked O
to O
episodic O
memory, O
semantic O
memory O
and O
temporality.ObjectiveThis O
study O
has O
two O
aims: O
(1) O
to O
investigate O
the O
influence O
of O
the O
temporal O
framework O
on O
tense O
production O
in O
French-speaking O
people O
with O
AD, O
and O
(2) O
to O
determine O
whether O
abilities O
in O
temporality, O
episodic O
memory O
and O
semantic O
memory O
predict O
tense O
marking O
in O
discourse O
among O
this O
population.MethodsTense O
production O
was O
assessed O
using O
a O
storytelling-in-sequence O
task O
administered O
to O
21 O
people O
with O
biologically O
probable O
AD O
(age: O
71.95; O
% O
female: O
57) O
and O
a O
control O
group.ResultsThe O
results O
showed O
that O
people O
with O
AD O
produced O
more O
verb O
forms O
in O
the O
present O
tense O
in O
the O
non-present O
conditions O
than O
in O
the O
control O
group, O
especially O
in O
the O
future O
condition. O
Temporality O
and O
semantic O
memory O
predict O
future O
tense O
production O
in O
this O
disease.ConclusionsThis O
study O
suggests O
that O
studying O
time O
reference O
in O
AD O
provides O
information O
about O
the O
ability O
to O
mentally O
travel O
through O
time O
in O
this O
population. O

Sickness-induced O
sleep O
is O
a O
behavior O
conserved O
across O
species O
that O
promotes O
recovery O
from O
illness, O
yet O
the O
underlying O
mechanisms O
are O
poorly O
understood. O
Here, O
we O
show O
that O
interleukin-6-like O
cytokine O
signaling O
from O
the O
<i>Drosophila</i> O
gut O
to O
brain O
glial O
cells O
regulates O
sleep. O
Under O
healthy O
conditions, O
this O
pathway O
promotes O
wakefulness. O
However, O
elevated O
gut O
cytokine O
signaling O
in O
response O
to O
oxidative O
stress O
- O
triggered O
by O
immune O
and O
inflammatory O
responses O
in O
the O
intestine O
- O
induces O
sleep. O
The O
cytokines O
Unpaired O
2 O
and O
-3 O
are O
upregulated O
by O
oxidative O
stress O
in O
enteroendocrine O
cells O
and O
activate O
JAK-STAT O
signaling O
in O
glial O
cells, O
including O
those O
of O
the O
blood-brain O
barrier O
(BBB). O
This O
activity O
maintains O
elevated O
sleep O
during O
oxidative-stress-induced O
intestinal O
disturbances, O
suggesting O
that O
the O
JAK-STAT O
pathway O
in O
glia O
inhibits O
wake-promoting O
signaling O
to O
facilitate O
sleep-dependent O
restoration O
under O
these O
conditions. O
We O
find O
that O
the O
enteric O
peptide O
Allatostatin O
A O
(AstA) O
enhances O
wakefulness, O
and O
during O
intestinal O
oxidative O
stress, O
gut-derived O
Unpaired O
2/3 O
inhibits O
AstA O
receptor O
expression O
in O
BBB O
glia, O
thereby O
sustaining O
an O
elevated O
sleep O
state O
during O
gut O
inflammation O
or O
illness. O
Taken O
together, O
our O
work O
identifies O
a O
gut-to-glial O
communication O
pathway O
that O
couples O
sleep O
with O
intestinal O
homeostasis O
and O
disease, O
enhancing O
sleep O
during O
intestinal O
sickness, O
and O
contributing O
to O
our O
understanding O
of O
how O
sleep O
disturbances O
arise O
from O
gastrointestinal O
disturbances. O

Sensory O
processing O
(SP) O
difficulties, O
such O
as O
heightened O
sensitivity O
to O
sensory O
input, O
have O
been O
linked O
to O
prolonged O
recovery O
and O
persistent O
post-concussive O
symptoms O
in O
adults O
following O
mild O
traumatic O
brain O
injury O
(mTBI). O
However, O
research O
on O
SP O
across O
different O
sensory O
inputs O
after O
pediatric O
mTBI O
is O
limited. O
This O
study O
examined O
SP O
patterns O
in O
children O
and O
adolescents O
aged O
6-17 O
years O
with O
mTBI O
at O
2 O
weeks O
and O
6 O
months O
post-injury. O
Caregivers O
of O
children O
aged O
6-11 O
years O
(<i>n</i> O
= O
120) O
completed O
the O
Short O
Sensory O
Profile O
- O
Dutch O
version O
(SSP-NL), O
while O
adolescents O
aged O
12-17 O
years O
(<i>n</i> O
= O
121) O
completed O
the O
Adolescent/Adult O
Sensory O
Profile O
- O
Dutch O
version O
(AASP-NL), O
and O
scores O
were O
compared O
to O
normative O
data. O
Multilevel O
latent O
class O
growth O
analysis O
(MLCGA) O
identified O
four O
SP O
classes O
in O
children: O
(1) O
taste O
and O
smell O
sensitivity, O
(2) O
general O
hypersensitivity, O
(3) O
general O
hyposensitivity, O
and O
(4) O
under O
responsiveness O
with O
auditory O
filtering O
issues. O
Class O
2 O
showed O
lower O
Child O
and O
Adolescent O
Scale O
of O
Participation O
(CASP) O
scores. O
SP O
changes O
over O
time O
were O
limited, O
with O
persistent O
issues O
(of O
hyper- O
and O
hyposensitivity) O
at O
6 O
months. O
For O
adolescents, O
two O
classes O
were O
found: O
(1) O
avoidant O
and O
passive, O
and O
(2) O
mild, O
with O
Class O
1 O
