brain O
injury O
(TBI). O
Cerebrovascular O
autoregulation O
supports O
stabilization O
of O
cerebral O
blood O
flow O
within O
the O
autoregulation O
range. O
Beyond O
the O
upper O
limit O
of O
this O
range, O
cerebral O
blood O
flow O
increases O
with O
increasing O
CPP, O
leading O
to O
increased O
risk O
of O
intracranial O
hypertension O
and O
blood-brain O
barrier O
disruptions. O
Based O
on O
the O
hypothesis O
that O
children O
are O
less O
sensitive O
to O
high O
CPP, O
we O
aimed O
to O
characterize O
the O
pediatric O
upper O
limit O
of O
autoregulation O
and O
the O
association O
between O
high O
CPP O
and O
outcome. O
Data O
acquired O
as O
part O
of O
the O
"Studying O
Trends O
of O
Autoregulation O
in O
Severe O
Head O
Injury O
in O
Paediatrics" O
(STARSHIP) O
study O
(a O
prospective, O
multicenter, O
observational O
study O
that O
enrolled O
135 O
children O
with O
TBI O
from O
July O
2018 O
to O
March O
2023) O
were O
explored. O
The O
association O
between O
different O
levels O
of O
CPP O
and O
the O
autoregulation O
proxy O
measure, O
the O
pressure O
reactivity O
index O
(PRx), O
were O
explored O
visually. O
The O
prognostic O
value O
of O
CPP O
was O
assessed O
by O
exploring O
overall O
averages, O
overall O
dose, O
hourly O
dose, O
and O
percentage O
time O
spent O
above O
specific O
thresholds. O
We O
employed O
univariable/multivariable O
(χ<sup>2</sup> O
tests, O
logistic O
regression, O
sliding O
dichotomy) O
and O
visual O
(heatmap) O
methods. O
No O
clear O
upper O
limit O
of O
autoregulation O
could O
be O
identified O
with O
PRx O
increasing O
beyond O
0.2 O
only O
with O
CPP O
values O
beyond O
100 O
mm O
