treatment O
strategies, O
and O
outcomes O
of O
brain O
abscesses O
caused O
by O
<i>Fusobacterium O
nucleatum</i> O
(<i>F. O
nucleatum</i>). O
After O
a O
detailed O
literature O
search O
using O
PubMed, O
12 O
cases O
were O
analyzed, O
containing O
a O
total O
of O
13 O
patients, O
over O
the O
period O
of O
the O
last O
24 O
years, O
between O
2000 O
and O
2024. O
All O
case O
reports O
and O
case O
series, O
including O
data O
relevant O
to O
brain O
abscess O
caused O
by O
<i>F. O
nucleatum</i>, O
were O
included, O
while O
letters O
to O
the O
editor O
and O
non-human O
studies O
were O
excluded. O
The O
main O
findings O
were O
male O
predominance O
(nine O
(69.23%)). O
The O
clinical O
presentations O
were O
headache O
(seven O
(53.85%)), O
fever O
(five O
(38.46%)), O
and O
neurological O
deficit O
(four O
(30.77%)), O
predominantly O
found O
in O
patients O
with O
poor O
oral O
health O
(seven O
(53.85%)), O
extracranial O
abscess O
sources O
with O
the O
presence O
of O
<i>F. O
nucleatum</i> O
(four O
(30.76%)), O
and O
alcohol O
consumption O
(four O
(30.76%)). O
Magnetic O
resonance O
imaging O
was O
the O
most O
commonly O
used O
radiological O
investigation O
in O
the O
majority O
of O
patients O
(nine O
(69.23%)). O
In O
biochemical O
investigations, O
blood O
cultures O
(eight O
(61.53%)) O
yielded O
negative O
results. O
However, O
brain O
abscess O
culture O
(four O
(30.76%)) O
as O
well O
as O
nucleic O
acid O
amplification O
test/polymerase O
chain O
reaction O
on O
culture-negative O
samples O
showed O
the O
presence O
of O
<i>F. O
nucleatum</i>. O
The O
majority O
of O
the O
treatment O
strategies O
were O
both O
surgical O
and O
medical. O
The O
surgical O
intervention O
involved O
craniotomy O
(three O
(23.07%)) O
and O
extraventricular O
drain O
placement, O
while O
medical O
management O
included O
courses O
of O
antibiotics O
such O
as O
metronidazole O
(eight O
(61.53%)), O
