this O
end, O
participants O
incidentally O
encoded O
everyday O
objects O
in O
VR O
and O
on O
a O
2D O
desktop O
in O
a O
within-subjects O
design, O
followed O
by O
unannounced O
old/new O
and O
source O
identification O
tasks. O
Our O
results O
indicate O
that O
encoding O
modality O
affects O
item O
memory O
only O
to O
a O
limited O
degree: O
Recognition O
memory O
performance, O
alongside O
the O
electrophysiological O
markers O
of O
item O
memory, O
i.e., O
the O
frontal O
and O
parietal O
old/new O
effects O
(FN400, O
LPC) O
and O
the O
theta O
band O
response, O
yielded O
comparable O
results O
for O
both O
engrams. O
Yet O
source O
memory O
differs O
depending O
on O
the O
encoding O
modality: O
The O
late O
posterior O
negativity O
indicated O
a O
shift O
towards O
recollection O
regarding O
the O
retrieval O
of O
VR-engrams O
compared O
to O
PC-engrams. O
This O
shift O
might O
result O
from O
attenuated O
familiarity O
with O
VR-engrams, O
particularly O
reflected O
in O
the O
alpha O
band O
and O
phase-amplitude O
coupling O
of O
theta O
and O
gamma O
band. O
In O
conclusion, O
