Despite O
extensive O
research O
into O
Alzheimer's O
disease O
(AD), O
few O
therapeutic O
strategies O
have O
successfully O
addressed O
its O
core O
pathology O
at O
the O
synaptic O
level. O
Small O
peptides O
represent O
a O
promising O
class O
of O
therapeutic O
agents O
capable O
of O
modulating O
key O
molecular O
pathways O
involved O
in O
amyloid O
toxicity, O
tau O
hyperphosphorylation, O
and O
synaptic O
degeneration. O
Their O
unique O
ability O
to O
cross O
biological O
barriers, O
interact O
with O
intracellular O
targets, O
and O
be O
modified O
for O
enhanced O
stability O
positions O
them O
as O
viable O
candidates O
for O
next-generation O
treatments O
targeting O
cognitive O
decline O
in O
AD.Small O
peptides O
show O
strong O
therapeutic O
potential O
yet O
face O
challenges O
in O
clinical O
application O
due O
to O
poor O
bioavailability O
and O
rapid O
enzymatic O
degradation. O
To O
deal O
with O
these O
limitations, O
various O
delivery O
strategies O
such O
as O
intranasal O
administration, O
nanoparticle O
encapsulation, O
and O
chemical O
modification O
have O
been O
developed. O
When O
combined O
with O
advanced O
delivery O
systems, O
small O
peptides O
hold O
significant O
promise O
for O
mitigating O
synaptic O
dysfunction O
and O
slowing O
the O
progression O
of O
Alzheimer's O
disease. O
In O
this O
review, O
we O
examine O
the O
mechanisms O
of O
action O
of O
four O
small O
peptides O
that O
demonstrate O
potential O
in O
alleviating O
Alzheimer's-related O
symptoms. O
We O
also O
evaluate O
the O
most O
effective O
delivery O
methods, O
emphasizing O
how O
these O
approaches O
enhance O
the O
peptides' O
therapeutic O
efficacy. O

