The O
association O
between O
ischemic O
stroke O
(IS) O
and O
malignancy O
is O
well O
established. O
Cancer-related O
strokes O
are O
predominantly O
embolic O
and O
classified O
as O
embolic O
strokes O
of O
undetermined O
source O
(ESUS). O
While O
malignancy-associated O
coagulopathy O
represents O
the O
primary O
pathogenic O
mechanism, O
neoplastic O
embolization O
of O
circulating O
tumor O
cells O
is O
another O
potential O
etiology, O
particularly O
in O
cases O
of O
cardiac O
and O
pulmonary O
malignancies. O
We O
report O
the O
case O
of O
a O
58-year-old O
man O
who O
presented O
with O
ESUS O
and O
concurrent O
multiple O
pulmonary O
nodules. O
Despite O
a O
comprehensive O
oncologic O
evaluation, O
including O
an O
ultrasound-guided O
bronchoscopic O
biopsy O
of O
the O
suspected O
lesion, O
no O
malignancy O
was O
detected. O
Several O
months O
later, O
the O
patient O
developed O
focal O
seizures, O
and O
brain O
magnetic O
resonance O
imaging O
(MRI) O
revealed O
multiple O
left O
frontoparietal O
space-occupying O
lesions O
with O
imaging O
features O
suggestive O
of O
brain O
metastases O
(BMs). O
A O
follow-up O
whole-body O
computed O
tomography O
(CT) O
scan O
confirmed O
a O
right O
upper O
lobe O
lung O
mass, O
which O
was O
diagnosed O
as O
pulmonary O
adenocarcinoma O
on O
subsequent O
mediastinal O
lymph O
node O
biopsy. O
The O
ischemic O
event O
may O
have O
contributed O
to O
the O
subsequent O
development O
of O
BMs O
through O
neoplastic O
embolization, O
allowing O
malignant O
cells O
to O
proliferate O
within O
the O
ischemic O
brain O
parenchyma. O
Alternatively, O
the O
stroke O
may O
have O
resulted O
from O
cancer-associated O
coagulopathy. O
In O
this O
context, O
post-stroke O
pathophysiological O
changes-including O
blood-brain O
barrier O
(BBB) O
disruption, O
neuroinflammation, O
hypoxia-induced O
angiogenesis, O
and O
extracellular O
matrix O
(ECM) O
remodeling-may O
have O
created O
a O
permissive O
microenvironment O
for O
tumor O
cell O
seeding O
and O
colonization, O
definable O
as O
a O
"pre-metastatic O
niche". O
ESUS O
can O
be O
the O
initial O
clinical O
manifestation O
of O
an O
undiagnosed O
malignancy, O
and O
IS O
itself O
may O
facilitate O
tumor O
dissemination, O
ultimately O
leading O
to O
BMs. O
This O
case O
underscores O
the O
importance O
not O
only O
of O
a O
thorough O
oncologic O
workup O
in O
